---
/**
 * THE GREEN INFRASTRUCTURE TOOLBOX — the sidebar's Scenarios pane.
 *
 * THIS FILE IS A MOVE, NOT AN EDIT. Every line of the template below arrived
 * verbatim from `<aside class="panel left">` in HeatMapStage.astro, which was
 * 1,050 lines and had to stop being the place where four components live. Same
 * sliders, same segmented controls, same score ring, same three pillars, same
 * ids, same comments — including the two that say "below", which now means the
 * stage's stylesheet rather than this file's. Nothing was tidied on the way
 * out, because a move mixed with an edit is a diff nobody can review: the
 * reviewer cannot tell which changed lines were the point and which were
 * incidental, so both halves go unchecked.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * THE IDS ARE LOAD-BEARING, AND THEY FAIL SILENTLY.
 *
 * heat-map-app.ts finds seventeen elements in here BY NAME at runtime —
 * `el('scoreArc')`, `querySelectorAll('#segPhase button')` — because the
 * instrument is vanilla TS mounted over server-rendered markup, with no
 * framework binding the two. There is therefore no compiler, no type and no
 * import edge holding the names together: rename one and the query returns
 * `null`, the optional chain shrugs, and that control simply stops responding.
 * Nothing throws. The page still looks right.
 *
 *   v1 ivTrees · v2 ivRoof · v4 ivFacades · segPhase segPath
 *   scoreArc scoreNum scoreTier scoreTxt · subs sGreen sCool sEff · scoreConf
 *
 * A second copy left behind in the stage would be just as quiet and slightly
 * worse: two elements with one id is invalid HTML, and `getElementById` hands
 * back whichever the parser reached first — the dead one. Both halves are
 * pinned by tests/unit/obos-shell.test.mjs, which asserts all seventeen are
 * here and that none of them is still there.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY THIS COMPONENT SHIPS NO <style>, WHERE THE OTHER THREE SCOPE.
 *
 * IconRail, ScopeSwitcher and LayerTree each carry their own scoped block, and
 * Task 2's guard holds them to it. This one is the documented exception, and
 * the exception is exempted there BY NAME.
 *
 * The toolbox's rules stay in HeatMapStage.astro's `is:global` block because
 * heat-map-app.ts WRITES CLASSES ONTO THIS MARKUP AT RUNTIME:
 *
 *   · `.on`, toggled across `#segPhase button` and `#segPath button` on every
 *     click (heat-map-app.ts:1837, 1840) — the segmented controls' entire
 *     visual state;
 *   · `.soft` on `#sCool` (heat-map-app.ts:1676), which greys the exposure
 *     pillar when its social-vulnerability input is unmeasured.
 *
 * Astro's scoping hash is applied at COMPILE time, to the selectors it can see
 * and the elements it rendered. A rule written here as `.seg button.on` would
 * compile to `.seg button.on[data-astro-cid-…]`, and the whole point of moving
 * the sheet would be to have the app's `classList.toggle('on', …)` keep
 * matching it — which it only does for as long as every element the app can
 * touch is one Astro itself emitted. That is true today and is exactly the
 * kind of invariant that stops being true the first time the app builds a
 * button, so the sheet stays global rather than resting on it.
 *
 * `#scoreArc` is the sharpest case and the reason nothing here was "cleaned
 * up": heat-map-app.ts:1656 does `setAttribute('stroke', tier.colour)` on it.
 * `stroke` is deliberately NOT set by any CSS rule — a `stroke:` declaration
 * anywhere would outrank the presentation attribute and permanently shadow the
 * runtime colour, so the ring would paint its pre-score cyan for ever while
 * every score still computed correctly. The element takes `currentColor` and
 * the stage's sheet sets `color`. Do not give this file a stylesheet that
 * "just" restates that.
 */
import { type ResolvedScope } from '../../../scripts/climate-engine/scope/resolve.ts';

/**
 * The scope, unchanged from what the stage already had.
 *
 * The moved markup reads exactly four things from it — `pathway.source`,
 * `pathway.options`, `pathway.initial` and `country.name` — all of them in the
 * warming-pathway control, whose whole argument is written out below. Passing
 * the resolved scope rather than those four fields keeps that control's
 * reasoning intact: it renders the scope's OWN table, and a narrower prop
 * would be the first step back towards a hardcoded one.
 */
interface Props {
  scope: ResolvedScope;
}

const { scope } = Astro.props;
---
<aside class="panel left">
      <div class="h">Green Infrastructure Toolbox</div>
      <div class="ctl"><label>Tree canopy corridors <span class="v" id="v1">0</span></label><input type="range" id="ivTrees" min="0" max="50" value="0" aria-label="Tree canopy corridors"></div>
      <div class="ctl"><label>Cool-roof albedo <span class="v" id="v2">0%</span></label><input type="range" id="ivRoof" min="0" max="100" value="0" step="5" aria-label="Cool-roof albedo, percent of roofs treated"></div>
      <div class="ctl"><label>Vertical green facades <span class="v" id="v4">0</span></label><input type="range" id="ivFacades" min="0" max="15" value="0" aria-label="Vertical green facades"></div>
      <div class="ctl"><label style="margin-bottom:.35rem">Diurnal phase</label><div class="seg" id="segPhase"><button class="on" data-p="now">Now</button><button data-p="peak">13:00 Peak</button><button data-p="night">22:00 Retained</button><button data-p="heatwave">Heatwave</button></div></div>
      {/* THE BUTTONS ARE THE SCOPE'S TABLE, DRAWN. Three were written out here -
          data-p="2025" | "ssp245" | "ssp585" - under a title citing an Indian paper,
          while `pathwayDelta` in heat-map-model.ts fails closed against whatever
          table the scope actually carries. Two countries, one hardcoded control: a
          country with a DIFFERENT populated table would render India's three and
          throw out of the click handler on the first press, and a country with an
          empty table would render three buttons that do nothing, under a citation
          for research it has not adopted. See `PathwayControl`. */}
      <div class="ctl"><label style="margin-bottom:.35rem" title={scope.pathway.source ?? undefined}>Warming pathway</label>{scope.pathway.options.length > 0
        ? <div class="seg" id="segPath">{scope.pathway.options.map((opt) => (
            <button class:list={{ on: opt.key === scope.pathway.initial }} data-p={opt.key}>{opt.label}</button>))}</div>
        /* NOT AN EMPTY <div class="seg">, which would render as a hairline box with
           nothing in it and read as a control still loading. An empty table is a
           DECLARED absence - no regional projection has been adopted - and that is a
           different fact from zero warming, so the control says which. */
        : <p class="segnone">No regional warming pathway has been adopted for {scope.country.name}, so there is nothing to project. This is a stated absence, not a projection of no warming.</p>}</div>
      <div class="score">
        <div class="g">
          {/* `stroke` is an SVG PRESENTATION ATTRIBUTE, not CSS, so `var(--surface2)`
              written here would not resolve — an attribute is parsed by the SVG
              attribute grammar, which has no custom properties. `currentColor` is
              the bridge: it defers to the element's own `color`, which IS CSS and
              takes the token (see `.score .g .track` / `#scoreArc` below).

              #scoreArc keeps its attribute writable on purpose — heat-map-app.ts
              setAttribute('stroke', tier.colour)s it once a score exists, and no CSS
              rule sets `stroke`, so the runtime value still wins. The token only
              paints the pre-score state, exactly as the cyan literal here did. */}
          <svg viewBox="0 0 36 36" aria-hidden="true"><circle class="track" cx="18" cy="18" r="15.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"></circle><circle id="scoreArc" cx="18" cy="18" r="15.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="97" stroke-dashoffset="97" stroke-linecap="round" transform="rotate(-90 18 18)"></circle></svg>
          <div class="num" id="scoreNum">—</div>
        </div>
        <div class="t"><b>Resilience Score</b><span class="tier" id="scoreTier">—</span><span id="scoreTxt">move a slider to intervene</span></div>
      </div>
      <!-- The three pillars, shown raw. A composite index is only trustworthy if
           you can see what it is made of; the Green Score published its three
           components for the same reason. Exposure and hazard are shown as their
           COMPLEMENTS (1 − EVI, 1 − THI) so that on every bar, higher is better —
           mixing polarities in one strip is how a reader misreads the worst ward
           as the best. -->
      <div class="subs" id="subs" title="DC-URS pillars, IPCC AR6 framing. Capacity: greenness, albedo and refuge access. Exposure: shown as 1 − EVI, so higher is better; driven by population, built density and social vulnerability, none of which any intervention can move. Hazard: shown as 1 − THI, from measured ECOSTRESS surface temperature.">
        <div><b id="sGreen">—</b><span>capacity</span></div>
        <div><b id="sCool">—</b><span>exposure</span></div>
        <div><b id="sEff">—</b><span>hazard</span></div>
      </div>
      <!-- Written by the app from unmeasured(), which reads the data file's own
           provenance. Deliberately no copy in this file for anyone to forget to
           delete: when socio.json lands the chip removes itself. -->
      <div class="conf indicative scoreconf" id="scoreConf" hidden></div>
    </aside>
